{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Follow-up: the docker restart's effect is now confirmed by the ALARM, not only by my own measurement.\n\n`rebar-docker-unaccounted-bytes` has **cleared** (it was in ALARM before the restart). The metric:\n\n    before restart   2,835,227,008 bytes  (2.84 GB)  -- above the 2 GiB alarm threshold\n    after  restart   1,421,580,704 bytes  (1.42 GB)  -- below it, alarm returned to OK\n\nRoot filesystem also eased: 61% -> 58%.\n\n## What did NOT clear, stated so it is not assumed\n\n`rebar-docker-buildkit-cache-high` is **still firing**, and its metric still reads a genuine 100.0 — this is a real breach, not missing data (I checked the datapoints rather than the alarm's StateReason text, which describes the transition and not the present).\n\nThe cap IS in effect — verified earlier from `daemon.json` (`builder.gc.maxUsedSpace = 5368709120`) against `dockerd ActiveEnterTimestamp`, with the daemon started after the policy was written. The likely reason the cache has not shrunk is that BuildKit's GC reclaims on build activity, and `rebar-autodeploy.service` has been idle since the restart, so no build has run to trigger it. That is a hypothesis from the timing, not a verified mechanism — the next autodeploy will settle it either way.\n\nSo the honest position: the restart delivered the orphan sweep immediately and verifiably, and the BuildKit ceiling is armed but has not yet been exercised. Do not read the remaining alarm as the cap having failed."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788587448287160001,"uuid":"ab75eaae-54df-4103-8f55-6bbab696188c"}